useful  technique to be able to understand what  is actually going on because not in  every case  loss of function is associated with a  low level of expression for a particular  gene so we'll talk about the differences  between gene  and transcript level analysis but we'll  also talk about how to look at that  detail by making sure that we are  actually developing a transcriptome from  our own data and only then going through  the process of annotation  now we will try to apply this in the  context of a biomedical research project  and so we'll  talk about how uh bioinformatics allows  us to essentially connect clinical and  omics data  how it allows us to address questions  around diagnostics and  applications of therapeutics so we will  kind of extend our understanding of  this expression of genes isoforms and  proteins  translation into proteins to then ask  more biomedical type questions from the  data that we will explore  the program will have several different  sessions we'll start from just the basic  logic of what i explained how is that  processing done and what are the  important steps in that pipeline and the  important steps are going to be  how the data is prepared with minimal  artifact so that our conclusions about  it at the very end are actually reliable  something that we can trust and so we'll  talk about different methods of  data preprocessing or cleaning  we'll talk about mapping how can we map  on the transcriptome on genome how do we  actually identify those alternative  splicing events etc and then we'll talk  about how can that data be quantified  and used to represent a difference  between conditions so in the most simple  experiment you will have  several replicas in one condition  several replicas in another condition  and because they have been  kind of produced in a controlled way you  can you know make biological  interpretation based on these  differentially expressed genes and we'll  talk about plotting uh volcano plots and  things like that but also about  downstream interpretation how do we  connect those differentially expressed  genes with gene sets  like pathways gene ontologies etc and  how do we make sense of what's actually  going on  now to do that we will have to cover all  of the steps from the basic alignment as  you see here with the reads aligned to  the reference genome to then  quantification  analysis of variance  and understanding of whether it is the  right approach to be able to compare  multiple uh conditions and how do we  actually go about more complex data sets  where instead of just two contrasts you  all of a sudden have many  how can you analyze that kind of data  and that is again bringing us back to  that question of data mining and  classification really being able to work  with larger and more complex data sets  and extract meaningful insights by using  non-linear methods of  machine learning and and feature  selection  so then we'll also talk about single  cell transcriptomics so an exciting  technique becoming very very popular  many cores are now adopting uh 10x  genomics and other machines to be able  to produce single cell data question is  what can we do with it and how do we do  it so that we are in control of that  analysis and we know how to ask  biological questions and actually get  answers to those so to do that we will  talk about  typical approaches so the standard  output being the umap or the t-sne  presents us with a clear picture  that unfortunately is not  trivial to explain because it really  contains a lot of information that has  been adapted to a  particular hyperplane and it's difficult  to connect back to biology so we'll look  at how we can use such methods of  dimensionality reduction to start asking  biological question going back to the  statistical comparisons that we've  explored in the bulk rna seq course and  how do we then can also ask additional  questions about for example pseudo time  how can we track trajectories where gene  expression levels actually represent a  continuous process  then we will look at the full pipeline  again from understanding of what is in  our data to then being able to ask  specific questions and then all the way  to downstream interpretation now all of  these are going to be given in the  context of several different project  examples and we'll talk about  patient-derived synographic models where  we will compare tumor and the  microenvironment we will talk about  immune cells  and ability to understand different  subtypes of cancer we'll talk about  macrophages how  the complex immune responses  could actually be both  detrimental and  supportive of  tumor development  and we'll also talk about early stage  cancer detection like biomarkers in  exosomes so a number of different types  of projects that we will look at will  offer also a collection of curated data  sets that you can kind of try to apply  some of the methods that we went over in  the program to something that is more  relevant to maybe your research  interests and we did call this  transcriptomics for  chronic diseases so those are the types  of examples that we're going to use but  if you would like to then use it to a  data set of your own that is also going  to be possible and we have several ways  that you can do that including talking  to someone that can guide you through  that process discuss your project with  you and help you kind of arrive at those  results
 

